#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_erf

#% text
# `inverse_erf(y)` gives the `x` with `erf(x) = y`, for `-1 < y < 1`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The inverse is odd; `inverse_erf(0)` is 0 and `inverse_erf(y)` tends to `inf` as `y` tends to 1.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values; other exact arguments stay unevaluated.

#% item
# Near 1 the argument `1 - y` loses digits in machine numbers; `inverse_erfc` of the small difference keeps them.

#% item
# `inverse_erf` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_erf(0.5)
#% output
#= 0.4769362762044699

#% cell
inverse_erf(0)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
inverse_erf(-0.9)
#% output
#= -1.1630871536766743

#% cell
n(inverse_erf(1/2), 30)
#% output
#= 0.47693627620446987338141835364313056

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The half-width of the central 95% interval of a standard normal distribution:

#% cell
sqrt(2.0)*inverse_erf(0.95)
#% output
#= 1.959963984540054

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `erf` undoes it:

#% cell
erf(inverse_erf(0.3))
#% output
#= 0.29999999999999993

#% text
# The relation to `inverse_erfc`:

#% cell
inverse_erf(0.4) - inverse_erfc(0.6)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Outside `(-1, 1)` there is no real value, and the call stays unevaluated:

#% cell
inverse_erf(1.5)
#% output
#= inverse_erf(1.5)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `erf`, `inverse_erfc`, `erfc`, `quantile`
